                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I played the first Orwell and left it with a very positive impression. It's a style of game and a set of themes that I think are seriously underserved in gaming, so I was always going to be interested in a follow-up.\n\nI'm giving this a cautious recommendation, but with a lot of caveats.\n\nThe biggest issue is that it's simply not as good as the first game. That's surprising to me, because I would have expected the developers to build on what worked and refine the formula. Instead, the story and overall experience feel less cohesive and less thoughtfully put together than the original.\n\nAt times, it almost feels like the success of the first game caught everyone by surprise and this sequel was pushed out quickly before the moment passed. Whether that's fair or not, that's the impression I came away with.\n\nThat doesn't mean it's bad. If you already love the Orwell formula, the themes it explores, and the style of investigative gameplay it offers, there's still plenty here to enjoy. That's ultimately why this still lands on the positive side for me.\n\nMy recommendation is simple:\n\nIf you've never played either game, start with the first Orwell. Absolutely start there before making up your mind about the series.\n\nIf you played the first game and loved it, then this is an easy pickup.\n\nBut if you played the first game, appreciated what it was doing, and felt you'd already gotten everything you wanted from the concept, then I'm not convinced Ignorance is Strength brings enough new ideas to justify the journey.\n\nSo, in short: play the first one. If you really like it, play this too.\n\nRecommended, but with a lot of asterisks attached.",
